{"event":"agent","text":"**Short answer:** Yes, the board's MRR is built up account by account, but it comes through `fct_mrr`, not `dim_accounts`. The two models have no direct relationship.\n\n**How the MRR figure is built**\n- `rpt_board_metrics.mrr_usd` is an aggregation of `fct_mrr.mrr_usd`, grouped by `month_start` [ods_lineage]. `arr_usd` aggregates the same column, and `net_new_mrr_usd` aggregates `fct_mrr.mrr_change_usd`. `paying_accounts` aggregates `fct_mrr.account_id` and is conditional on `mrr_usd`.\n- `fct_mrr` has one row per account per calendar month in which the account had MRR or lost it [repo, reviewed]. So the board figure is a sum of account-month rows.\n- `fct_mrr` is built from `int_subscription_months` (subscription-level Stripe data), which has `account_id` [ods_lineage]. `account_id` comes from `stg_stripe__customers`, which reads the Stripe customer's `account_id` metadata.\n\n**`dim_accounts` is not in the chain**\n- It has one row per `account_id` [ods_describe_entity, tested]. Its only tested relationship is `fct_invoices` to `dim_accounts`.\n- `rpt_board_metrics` has no declared or tested relationships, and lineage shows no edge from `dim_accounts` into it or into `fct_mrr`.\n- This means the board MRR does not apply the `dim_accounts` filters. The team context says paying customers should exclude `is_internal`, free and deleted accounts [repo, reviewed].\n\n**Uncertain, and needs checking**\n- Whether `fct_mrr` excludes internal and test accounts is unknown. Lineage shows no join to `dim_accounts` or `int_account_identity`, so internal accounts may be included. Could you confirm, or should I run `ods_get_node` on `fct_mrr` to look at its filters?\n- `int_subscription_months` is opaque to lineage (the `range` table function isn't supported), so the lineage upstream of `fct_mrr` is uncertain.\n- `rpt_board_metrics` has no known grain or key. The team context says one row per calendar month [repo, reviewed], but nothing tests it.\n- Open issue DATA-412: Stripe plan changes show in `fct_mrr` as churn followed by reactivation instead of contraction or expansion. This affects `net_new_mrr_usd`. It should not change total `mrr_usd` much, but I haven't verified that.\n- ARR has two conflicting reviewed definitions: MRR × 12, or signed contract value including subscriptions that haven't started. The `arr_usd` column aggregates `fct_mrr.mrr_usd`, which fits the first definition. Which one does the board intend?"}
{"event":"human","text":"Is that the same as saying one is built from the other in dbt?"}
{"event":"agent","text":"No. They are two different ideas, and only one of them holds here.\n\n- **Built from, in dbt (lineage):** `rpt_board_metrics` is built from `fct_mrr`, and `fct_mrr` is built from `int_subscription_months` [ods_lineage node edges]. `dim_accounts` is not upstream of either, so `rpt_board_metrics` is not built from `dim_accounts`.\n- **Built up account by account (grain):** this describes the rows being summed, not a dbt dependency. `fct_mrr` has one row per account per month [repo, reviewed], and the report sums those rows. The account detail comes through `fct_mrr`'s own `account_id`, which originates in Stripe customer metadata.\n- **Related (join or key):** this is a third thing. `rpt_board_metrics` has no `account_id` column, so there is nothing to join it to `dim_accounts` on [ods_describe_entity]. `dim_accounts` can only be joined to `fct_mrr` through `account_id`. No relationship between those two is declared or tested in the project, so that join would be unconfirmed.\n\nA model can be built from another without joining to it. Here, the MRR figure is account-level in its inputs, but `dim_accounts` plays no part in producing it."}
